## SECTION 5: RULE AUTHORING STANDARDS

### RA1 — Rule Structure
Every rule must:
1. Subclass `DataQualityRule` (syntactic/semantic) or follow the stateful pattern in `cross_record.py`
2. Set `violation_type` class attribute
3. Implement `evaluate(ctx: RuleContext) -> Optional[Violation]`
4. Return `Violation` on failure, `None` on pass
5. Handle ALL edge cases: `None`, NaN, wrong type, parse error — before the main check
6. Include unit tests covering all failure modes

### RA2 — Violation Object Completeness
Every `Violation` must include:
- `rule_id`, `rule_name`, `entity_id`, `entity_type`, `severity`, `violation_type`
- `details` dict: field name, actual value, reason code, computed values
- `expected` dict: what was expected
- `record_snapshot`: the full event dict
- `detected_at`: timestamp
- `processing_latency_ms`: latency (NOT hardcoded 0 — compute from event-time)

### RA3 — No Silent Pass-Through
Every field access must have explicit guards:
- `None` → `Violation(reason="NULL")`
- NaN → `Violation(reason="NAN")` via `math.isnan()`
- Wrong type → `Violation(reason="TYPE_MISMATCH")`
- Parse error → `Violation(reason="PARSE_ERROR")`

### RA4 — Edge Case Test Pattern
Every rule test class must have: `test_valid_*`, `test_null_*`, `test_nan_*`, `test_type_mismatch_*`, `test_out_of_range_*`, `test_edge_case_*`.

### RA5 — Haversine for GPS
Use `haversine_distance()` from `cross_record.py` for GPS distance. Do NOT approximate with Euclidean.

