## SECTION 7: PAPER WRITING STANDARDS

### PW1 — Claim Classification
Every claim: Tier 1 (verified), Tier 2 (estimated+label), or Tier 3 (unmeasurable+label). Never present Tier 2 as Tier 1.

### PW2 — Positioning
StreamDQ is a **research and education platform**, not a production competitor to Stream DaQ or Great Expectations. Do NOT claim production-readiness, fault tolerance, or scalability.

### PW3 — Transparent Comparisons
Structured comparison table: architecture, adaptive thresholds, cross-record detection, latency, maturity. Compare against GE, Soda, dbt, Stream DaQ, AutoDQM.

### PW4 — Section Completeness
Every section must have: (1) problem statement, (2) approach, (3) implementation, (4) results with CI, (5) limitations, (6) related work.

### PW5 — Active Voice for Contributions
"We implement X" not "X is proposed." "StreamDQ validates" not "validation is achieved."

### PW6 — Cite Before Claiming
Every claim about prior work must have a citation. Remove numbers that can't be cited.

### PW7 — Tables Over Prose
Use structured tables for framework comparisons, rule summaries, benchmark results.

### PW8 — Honest Limitations
- Adaptive thresholds only work on driver (not distributed)
- Layer 2 state not checkpointed
- Benchmark numbers are estimates until measured
- Duplicate injection bug affects CRS003 recall
- Single dataset (NYC TLC + NYC MTA Bus) — may not generalize

